### 📝 Description

# `UPTIME_KUMA_HIDE_LOG` does not work for `warn` level due to case mismatch in `Logger.log()`

> **Disclosure:** This bug was reproduced manually. The root cause analysis, suggested fix, and text of this report were written with AI assistance.



### Root cause

In [`[src/util.ts](https://github.com/louislam/uptime-kuma/blob/master/src/util.ts)`](https://github.com/louislam/uptime-kuma/blob/master/src/util.ts), the `Logger` class has a case mismatch between how `hideLog` is populated and how it is checked.

**Constructor** — parses the env var and stores entries under **lowercase** keys:

```typescript
hideLog: Record<string, string[]> = {
    info: [],   // lowercase
    warn: [],   // lowercase
    error: [],  // lowercase
    debug: [],  // lowercase
};

constructor() {
    if (typeof process !== "undefined" && process.env.UPTIME_KUMA_HIDE_LOG) {
        const list = process.env.UPTIME_KUMA_HIDE_LOG.split(",").map(v => v.toLowerCase());
        for (const pair of list) {
            const values = pair.split(/_(.*)/s);
            if (values.length >= 2) {
                this.hideLog[values[0]].push(values[1]);
                // values[0] is "warn" (lowercase) → pushes to this.hideLog["warn"]
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**`log()` method** — checks using the **uppercase** `level` parameter:

```typescript
log(module: string, level: "INFO" | "WARN" | "ERROR" | "DEBUG", ...msg: unknown[]) {
    if (this.hideLog[level] && this.hideLog[level].includes(module.toLowerCase())) {
        return;
    }
    // level is "WARN" (uppercase), but the key in hideLog is "warn" (lowercase)
    // this.hideLog["WARN"] → undefined → check is skipped → log is never hidden
}
```

`this.hideLog["WARN"]` is always `undefined` because the dictionary only has lowercase keys (`"warn"`). The `&&` short-circuits, so the hide check silently does nothing.

### Suggested fix

In the `log()` method, lowercase the `level` before looking it up in `hideLog`:

```typescript
const levelLower = level.toLowerCase();
if (this.hideLog[levelLower] && this.hideLog[levelLower].includes(module.toLowerCase())) {
    return;
}
```

Alternatively, store the `hideLog` keys in uppercase to match the `level` type signature — but lowercasing the lookup is the smaller, safer change since the constructor already normalizes input to lowercase.

